Om Sai Aadarsha Clinic — AI Studio Build Prompt (Revised)
Oct 26, 2026 · @Mithlesh Kumar  Das
1. How to work
You are building a real, working application for Om Sai Aadarsha Clinic: a bilingual (English / Nepali) public website, a patient portal with request-based bookings and secure lab-report access, and a private clinic-management CMS. Act as a senior full-stack engineer who also owns security and UX decisions.
Follow these rules throughout:
1. Inspect before changing. If the project already contains files, read its structure, dependencies and current behaviour first and report what you found. Extend working code; do not rewrite it without a stated reason.
2. One stage at a time. Build in the stages of Section 14. Finish a stage, run its checks, send the stage report, then stop. Do not start the next stage until told to continue.
3. Real implementations only. Anything you call working must use real persistence, real authentication, real server-side authorization and real file handling. Never fake a protected or persistent feature with hardcoded arrays, localStorage, or checks that exist only in the browser.
4. Report honestly. If a capability cannot run in this environment (for example private object storage or email delivery), build the integration boundary (an interface plus configuration), label it NOT CONFIGURED, and say exactly what must be supplied. Never present a stub as finished.
5. Use these status labels in every report: Implemented and tested, Implemented, not tested, Boundary only, Not started.
6. Do not invent clinic facts. Follow Section 13. Placeholders must look like placeholders; sample data exists only in development and is removable with one command.
7. Decide small things yourself. Ask a question only when a missing decision materially affects architecture, security, or the clinic's workflow. State each non-trivial decision and its reason in the stage report.
8. Keep it simple. Add no dependency, abstraction, service or feature that this prompt does not need. If a requirement conflicts with the platform, say so and propose the smallest workable alternative instead of silently dropping it.
9. When requirements conflict, prefer this order: patient-data security, then booking correctness, then bilingual completeness, then usability, then visual polish.
2. Product and fixed decisions
Build one cohesive application with three surfaces that share one database and one authorization layer: the public website, the patient portal, and the staff CMS at /sai-clinic-management. The clinic's core need is simple: patients request doctor appointments, lab tests and other services online; reception reviews and confirms; patients sign in to check statuses and download released lab reports.
The decisions below are fixed. If one looks technically impossible, explain why and propose the smallest alternative. Never substitute silently.
• Languages: English and Nepali (नेपाली) across the public site, patient portal, booking flows, validation messages, status labels and clinic content.
• Request-based booking: a submission is a request, never a confirmed appointment, until reception confirms it. Every patient-facing booking screen says so.
• Patient accounts are required to submit a booking and to see statuses and reports.
• CMS route: /sai-clinic-management. It is protected by real authentication and server-side authorization. A hidden URL is not a security measure. The route never appears in the public navbar, footer, sitemap, robots.txt or the patient dashboard.
• Lab reports are delivered online. Authorized staff upload and release them. A patient can open only released reports that belong to a patient profile managed by their own account.
• No automated booking notifications or reminders (no SMS, email, push or WhatsApp about bookings or reports). Patients check the portal. Emails used strictly for authentication (verification, password reset, staff invitation) are allowed and kept separate.
• No online payments: no gateway, checkout or payment dependency. Prices are informational.
• Reception manages bookings. Launch roles are Super Admin, Reception and Patient. The permission model must let the clinic add doctor or lab-staff roles later without redesign.
• Clinic facts are never invented (Section 13).
• Existing clinic software is unknown. Do not build integrations or migrations. In the Stage 0 blueprint, note where a patient or catalogue import boundary would fit.
3. Scope and priorities
Core. These must work end to end before anything else is polished: authentication and roles; doctor, lab-test and service catalogues managed in the CMS; request-based bookings with reception management; the patient dashboard; secure lab-report upload, release and access; the audit log.
Supporting. Build these after Core works: homepage and clinic-information management; About and FAQ; the blog; SEO metadata and structured data; banners and featured content; tooling that shows incomplete Nepali translations.
Non-goals. Do not add these or their dependencies: online payments; automated notifications or reminders; telemedicine; prescriptions; electronic medical records; pharmacy or inventory; AI diagnosis or AI-generated medical content; analytics platforms; a drag-and-drop page builder; microservices; native mobile apps; report generation (the app only delivers files that staff provide).
4. Platform and technology
Platform check (Stage 0, before any application code). Build mode changes quickly, so verify what this environment can actually provide and report each item as Confirmed (you saw it work or saw it documented here) or Assumed. Check: a server-side runtime; server-rendered pages (Next.js or an equivalent); database options (PostgreSQL, Firestore); sign-in methods (email and password, Google, others); private file storage; sending authentication email; secrets management; deployment target and region; export to GitHub or ZIP.
For orientation only, Google's documentation describes a React front end by default, a Node.js server runtime with secrets and npm, a Firebase integration (Firestore and Authentication, documented with Google sign-in), a Cloud SQL for PostgreSQL integration, and deployment to Cloud Run. Verify these; do not rely on them.
Preferred stack.
• TypeScript throughout, with Next.js (App Router), React and Tailwind CSS.
• PostgreSQL with Prisma or Drizzle. Migrations are committed.
• A maintained authentication solution. It hashes passwords. Never write custom cryptography.
• Server-side validation of every input with a schema library such as Zod.
• A standard localization library such as next-intl.
• Private object storage for lab reports, behind a storage interface.
Decision rules.
1. Database. Use PostgreSQL for core records, because booking conflicts, ownership checks and audit history need transactions and constraints. Use the platform's PostgreSQL integration if it exists; otherwise any managed PostgreSQL through a DATABASE_URL secret. Use Firestore for core records only if PostgreSQL is impossible. In that case say so in Stage 0 and explain how you will keep conflict checks transactional, enforce ownership on the server, and deny all direct client access through security rules.
2. Rendering. Serve public pages as server-rendered or pre-rendered HTML, with crawlable text, metadata and structured data for each URL. The SEO and bilingual-URL requirements depend on this. A client-only single-page app does not satisfy it.
3. Patient sign-in. Patients need self-registration, email verification and password reset. If the platform's built-in sign-in supports only Google accounts, treat that as incomplete: add email and password, or report what is missing. Google sign-in may be offered as an extra for patients, never as the only path.
4. Server-only data access. The browser never reads or writes the database or file storage directly. Every server read or write checks the session and the permission.
5. Report storage. Reports live in a private bucket or container with no public access, behind a ReportStorage interface (put, openStream, delete). Local disk is allowed only in development and must be marked NOT FOR PRODUCTION, because container hosts often have ephemeral disks.
6. Portability. Keep a standard project: configuration through environment variables with a .env.example, committed migrations, an idempotent seed script, and a README covering setup, deployment and restore. The clinic must be able to change hosts.
7. Missing credentials. If a service needs credentials you do not have, build the boundary and mark it NOT CONFIGURED. Never fake it.
