5. Public website and bilingual content
Navbar (fixed information architecture).
• Far left: the clinic logo and the name "Om Sai Aadarsha Clinic". Both link to Home.
• Main navigation, in this order: Home, Doctors, Medical Services, Lab Tests, Explore. Explore is a dropdown containing About, FAQ and Health Information (the blog).
• Rightmost: the English / नेपाली switch, then the primary button Book a Session.
• The CMS never appears in the navbar.
• The Explore dropdown works with mouse, keyboard (Enter, Space, Escape, arrow keys) and touch. On mobile, a menu button opens a panel with every item (Explore shown as a group), the language switch, and a prominent Book a Session button that stays visible.
• The language switch keeps the visitor on the current page when a translation exists. Otherwise it goes to the other language's home page with a short notice.
• The navbar has no sign-in item and must stay as specified. Give patients account access through the footer ("Patient login / My account"), the Book a Session page ("My bookings and reports" when signed in, "Sign in or register" when not), and the foot of the mobile menu.
Book a Session opens a booking hub: doctor appointment, lab tests, or other service. A signed-out visitor is asked to sign in or register, then returns to the same flow with their selection intact.
Public pages.
• Home: hero with the booking action; quick links to Doctors, Medical Services and Lab Tests; featured doctors and services chosen in the CMS; selected lab tests; clinic information, approved images and location from settings; FAQ preview; latest articles; footer with approved contact details, navigation, language options and privacy link. A section with no content is hidden. Never fill it with invented content.
• Doctors: searchable directory and individual pages with name, approved photograph, qualifications, specialty, biography, consultation fee (only if confirmed) and a booking action.
• Medical Services: catalogue and detail pages with description, fee, availability and booking type. Distinguish "appointment request" services from "informational only" services.
• Lab Tests: searchable catalogue filtered by category. Detail pages show description, approved price, clinic-approved preparation instructions, sample or collection information and availability. A patient can select several tests and start one lab booking.
• Explore: About, FAQ, and the Health Information blog (listing and articles). Articles carry draft, in-review and published states. Show "reviewed by" or dates only when staff entered them. Never present unreviewed health information as clinic-approved.
• Supporting pages: patient login, registration, account recovery, booking flows, dashboard, privacy policy, booking terms, confirmation pages, and 404, error and access-denied pages.
Localization.
• URLs: every public URL carries a locale prefix, /en/... or /ne/.... The bare / redirects once to the visitor's last chosen language, otherwise English. Do not choose language by IP address. Slugs use Latin script and are shared across languages. Emit hreflang (en, ne, x-default) and canonical tags, and only for translations that exist. The CMS stays at /sai-clinic-management with no locale prefix.
• No hardcoded text. All interface text lives in message files (en.json, ne.json) under stable keys. This includes validation messages, status labels, empty and error states, and authentication email text.
• Interface strings: you may draft the Nepali interface text. List every drafted Nepali string in docs/TRANSLATION-REVIEW.md for review by a native speaker.
• Clinic content (doctor biographies, service and test descriptions, preparation instructions, FAQs, articles, About) is entered by staff in each language. Never machine-translate it. Never invent translations of medical guidance. If a Nepali version is missing, show the English text with a visible note that Nepali is not yet available, and mark the item as an incomplete translation in the CMS.
• Nepali presentation: use a Devanagari-capable font such as Noto Sans Devanagari with comfortable line height. Store all date-times in UTC and display them in Asia/Kathmandu. Make week start and weekly holiday configurable; do not assume a Saturday–Sunday weekend. Display dates in Gregorian (AD) and route all date formatting through one helper so Bikram Sambat (BS) display can be added later (Section 13).
6. Accounts and authentication
Patient accounts.
• Registration collects full name, email, mobile number and password, plus consent to the privacy policy and booking terms (store the document version and timestamp). Collect nothing else at registration.
• Email verification is required before a patient can submit a booking. An unverified patient can sign in and sees a verification prompt.
• Sign-in uses email and password. Login, registration and password-reset responses must not reveal whether an email address is registered. Rate-limit by IP address and by account, with a temporary lockout after repeated failures.
• Password reset uses a single-use, short-lived token. A successful reset ends the account's other sessions.
• Sessions use HttpOnly, Secure, SameSite cookies, CSRF protection on state-changing requests, idle and absolute timeouts, and server-side invalidation at logout.
• No self-service account deletion in the MVP. Staff can disable an account. Booking and report records follow the clinic's retention policy (Section 13).
Patient profiles (self and family). One account may manage several patient profiles: "myself" (created at registration) and family members such as a child or parent. A profile has a name, date of birth, optional sex, optional phone and a relationship label. Every booking belongs to exactly one profile. A report belongs to the same profile as its booking. The account that manages a profile sees that profile's bookings and released reports, and nobody else does. Cap profiles per account at a configurable number (default 6).
Walk-in patients and claim codes (build in Stage 4). Reception can create a profile for someone with no account, such as a walk-in lab patient. That profile is unlinked, and no report can be released for it. To link it, reception verifies the patient's identity in person under the clinic's procedure and generates a one-time claim code. The code is random, expires after a configurable period, is stored hashed, and is shown once. The patient signs in, enters the code under "Add a profile", and the profile attaches to their account. Record who verified the identity and when.
Staff accounts.
• There is no public staff registration.
• Create the first Super Admin with a one-time bootstrap command that reads credentials from environment variables, forces a password change at first login, and refuses to run if a Super Admin already exists.
• A Super Admin creates staff accounts and assigns roles. The new staff member receives an invitation link, or a temporary password that must be changed at first login. Disabling a staff account ends its sessions immediately.
• Staff sign in at /sai-clinic-management/login. Patient accounts can never access the CMS: check the role on the server for every CMS page and API call. Keep staff and patient accounts separate, with different email addresses.
• Offer multi-factor authentication (TOTP) for staff if the authentication solution supports it natively, and require it for Super Admin where available. Otherwise list it as recommended hardening. Use shorter staff session timeouts than patient timeouts, and make logout prominent for shared reception computers.
Authentication email. Use one sendAuthEmail() function behind an email-provider boundary, only for verification, password reset and staff invitation. Without credentials in development, print the link to the server console. Without credentials in production, mark verification and reset NOT CONFIGURED and show a clear admin warning. Never log tokens in production.
7. Bookings
Rules for every booking type.
• The patient must be signed in and email-verified, and must choose which patient profile the booking is for.
• A submission is a request. The confirmation screen says so in plain words ("Request received. It is not confirmed until reception confirms it.") and shows a reference number.
• The reference number is random, non-sequential and human-friendly (for example OSA-7K4Q9X). It is a label only and never grants access to anything.
• Collect only what is needed: profile, preferred date and time, a short optional note, a contact phone if different, and service-specific answers.
• Validate on the server: the preferred time is not in the past, falls inside the bookable window (configurable minimum lead time and maximum days ahead), and is not on a clinic closure or doctor-unavailable date.
• Limit abuse: rate-limit submissions and cap each patient's open requests (pending or reschedule_proposed) at a configurable number, default 5.
• Keep staff notes (internal) separate from patient messages (shown to the patient, such as a decline reason).
• Write a status-history row for every change: from, to, actor, time, patient message, internal note.
Doctor appointments. The patient selects a doctor and sees slots computed from the doctor's weekly schedule and unavailable dates, minus already-confirmed appointments. The patient picks a preferred slot, completes the details and submits. Pending requests do not block a slot; several patients may request the same one. Slot length is set per doctor. Store the doctor's name and displayed fee on the appointment as a snapshot.
Lab bookings. The patient selects one or more bookable tests, reviews approved prices and clinic-approved preparation instructions, chooses a preferred visit time within the lab's configurable hours, and submits. Show the price total as an estimate and say final charges are set by the clinic. Store each test as a line item with a snapshot of its name (both languages), price and preparation text at request time. Do not assume home collection; offer it only where the clinic enables it for a test. The completed status means the visit or sample collection is done. It does not mean a report exists. Report availability comes only from the report record (Section 8).
Other services. Reuse the appointment request flow. Each service has a booking mode set in the CMS: request or informational (no booking). A request service may define a few simple extra questions (label in both languages, text or choice, required or not). Do not build a general form builder.
Statuses. Store stable codes: pending, confirmed, reschedule_proposed, declined, cancelled, completed, no_show. Show English and Nepali labels from the message files.
