Because no notifications are sent, a reschedule_proposed booking shows an "Action needed" banner in the patient dashboard and appears in a "waiting for patient" list on the staff dashboard. A proposal does not hold the slot.
Conflict rule. Two appointments conflict when they have the same doctor, overlapping time ranges, and both are confirmed. Enforce this in the database (for example a PostgreSQL exclusion constraint on doctor and time range for confirmed rows) or inside a serializable transaction. An application-level check alone is not enough, because two staff members can confirm at the same moment. On conflict, tell staff which booking holds the time. When staff review a request, show other requests for the same doctor and time so they can choose. Doctor-unavailable dates and clinic closures also block confirmation. Lab bookings have no doctor conflict; apply a capacity limit per time window only if the clinic configures one.
Patient dashboard. Sections: Upcoming, Past, Lab bookings, Reports, Profiles, Account. Each booking shows its reference, type, profile, requested and confirmed times, the status label in the current language, the patient message, the status history, and the actions allowed to the patient (cancel; accept or decline a proposal). A banner explains that a request is not confirmed until reception confirms it. Every query filters by the signed-in account's own profiles on the server.
8. Laboratory reports
This feature delivers finished files that clinic staff provide. It does not generate reports, interpret results or diagnose anyone.
Report record. Store one row per uploaded file version, with the lab booking, the patient profile, a version number, a status, file metadata, and who uploaded, released or revoked it and when. Statuses are draft, released, revoked and superseded.
Staff workflow.
1. Staff find the lab booking by reference or by patient search. Only a confirmed or completed lab booking can receive a report.
2. Staff upload a finalized PDF. Beside the upload, the app shows the patient's name, date of birth and the booking reference, and requires a ticked confirmation: "I have verified that this report belongs to this patient and booking." Store who confirmed it and when.
3. Staff save it as draft or release it. A user without the reports.release permission can only save drafts. A user with that permission reviews and releases.
4. Release is allowed only if the patient profile is linked to a patient account (Section 6). Otherwise the app explains how to link it with a claim code.
5. A released report appears in the patient's dashboard under Reports and on the related booking.
6. To correct a report, staff upload a new version and release it. In the same transaction, the earlier released version becomes superseded. The patient sees only the latest released version.
7. To withdraw a report, staff with release permission revoke it with a required internal reason. The patient loses access immediately and sees "This report is temporarily unavailable. Please contact the clinic."
Storage and delivery.
• Accept PDF only by default, with a configurable size limit (default 10 MB). Validate the file signature, not just the extension or the declared MIME type. Reject everything else.
• Store each file under a random, non-guessable key. Never put a patient name, phone number or original filename in the key. Keep the sanitized original filename as metadata and record a SHA-256 checksum.
• Never create a public or permanent URL for a report. Patients download through an authenticated server endpoint that (a) checks the session, (b) checks that the report is released and belongs to a profile managed by that account, and only then (c) streams the file or issues a signed URL that expires within a few minutes.
• Send Cache-Control: private, no-store, X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff and a generated download filename such as lab-report-OSA-7K4Q9X.pdf.
• Return the same "not found" response for a report that does not exist and a report the user may not see, so existence is never revealed.
• Keep report files and endpoints out of sitemaps, search indexes, analytics and logs.
• If malware scanning is available in this environment, scan on upload. If not, list it as a known gap in the Stage 4 report.
Audit. Write an append-only audit entry for upload, staff view, release, supersede, revoke, patient download, denied access attempts and claim-code linking. Entries hold identifiers, actor, action, timestamp and a request ID, never file contents or health details.
Permissions. The keys are reports.view, reports.upload, reports.release and reports.revoke (defaults in Section 9). The clinic decides who uploads and who releases. Until it does, Reception can upload and save drafts, and only Super Admin can release and revoke.
Retention. Reports are retained. The MVP has no delete function. Revoking hides a report but keeps the record.
9. CMS and roles
Route protection.
• Every page, API route and server action under /sai-clinic-management requires an authenticated staff session and a permission check on the server. Hiding a button in the interface is cosmetic only.
• An unauthenticated visitor reaches only /sai-clinic-management/login. A signed-in patient who requests the CMS gets the generic access-denied page.
• Do not list the route in the navbar, footer, sitemap, robots.txt or the patient dashboard. Do not add a Disallow line for it in robots.txt, because that would publish the path. Send X-Robots-Tag: noindex, nofollow and Cache-Control: no-store on every CMS response instead.
• Rate-limit the staff login. The CMS interface is in English. Content forms edit the English and Nepali fields side by side.
Modules. Build each as simple structured forms and searchable lists, with loading, empty, success and error states. Do not build a page builder.
• Dashboard (Core): pending requests; upcoming confirmed appointments; new lab requests; bookings waiting for a patient; reports in draft; recent audit activity.
• Bookings (Core): search and filter by status, type, doctor, date, reference and patient; detail view; confirm, decline, propose a new time, cancel, complete, mark no-show; internal notes; status history.
• Doctors (Core): bilingual profile, photograph, qualifications, specialty, fee, weekly schedule, slot length, unavailable dates, visibility.
• Medical services (Core): bilingual content, fee, availability, booking mode, optional extra questions, publish and deactivate.
• Lab catalogue (Core): tests with categories, bilingual name, description and preparation text, price, collection information, availability and visibility. A discontinued test accepts no new bookings and stays in history.
• Patients (Core): search by name, phone or email; view profiles and booking history; disable an account; create an unlinked profile; issue a claim code. Reception sees only what desk work needs.
• Lab reports (Core): as in Section 8.
• Clinic settings (Core): clinic name, logo, contact details, address and directions, opening hours, closures and holidays, booking window and cancellation cutoff, week start and weekly holiday, language settings, public contact information.
• User management (Core, Super Admin only): create and disable staff, assign roles and permissions, view audit history.
• Website content (Supporting): homepage sections, About, FAQ, blog articles (draft, in review, published), banners, images, featured items, and page metadata per language.
Translation status. Every bilingual item shows Complete, Missing Nepali or Missing English. Staff can list and filter incomplete items. Publishing with a missing translation is allowed, with a visible warning, because the public site falls back to the other language.
