10. Data model
Design a normalized relational schema. Adjust it where you can justify a better design, and record the change in the Stage 0 blueprint. Include at least these entities.
Entity
Purpose and key points
User
Login identity for patients and staff: email, status (active or disabled), email-verified time, type (patient or staff), preferred language. Password data is managed by the authentication solution.
Role, StaffProfile
A role holds default permission keys. A staff profile links a user to a role and holds per-account added or removed permission keys.
PatientProfile
A person who receives care: name, date of birth, optional sex, phone, relationship label. managing_user_id is null until linked. Records who verified identity and when.
ClaimCode
One-time code for linking a walk-in profile: profile, hashed code, expiry, used time, creator.
Doctor, DoctorSchedule, DoctorUnavailability
Bilingual profile, photo, fee, slot length, visibility; weekly availability windows; unavailable date ranges.
ClinicClosure
Clinic-wide closed dates.
MedicalService, ServiceQuestion
Bilingual service content, fee, availability, booking mode (request or informational), optional extra questions.
LabTestCategory, LabTest
Bilingual name, description, preparation text and collection information; price; availability; visibility.
Appointment
Reference, profile, doctor or service, start and end, status, snapshots of name and fee, patient note, patient message, and service answers.
LabBooking, LabBookingItem
Reference, profile, preferred time, status; one item per test with snapshots of name (both languages), price and preparation text.
BookingStatusEvent
History of every status change: booking, from, to, actor, patient message, internal note, time.
LabReport
One row per file version: lab booking, profile, version, status, storage key, file metadata, checksum, and uploader, confirmer, releaser and revoker with times.
Page, Article, FAQ, Banner, MediaAsset
Managed website content with bilingual fields, publish state and metadata.
ClinicSetting
Typed settings: contact details, address, opening hours, booking window, cancellation cutoff, week start, weekly holiday, language options.
AuditLog
Append-only record of sensitive actions: actor, action, entity type and ID, time, request ID.
Rules.
• Bilingual fields: use paired columns such as name_en and name_ne, which are simple and queryable. Derive a translation status from them.
• Snapshots: bookings copy the names, fees, prices and preparation text shown at request time, so later catalogue edits never rewrite history.
• No hard deletes of referenced records. Deactivate catalogue entries instead. Use restrictive foreign keys.
• Ownership by constraint: make it impossible in the database to attach a report to a profile that does not match its lab booking, for example a composite foreign key on lab booking and profile.
• Conflict constraint: enforce non-overlapping confirmed appointments per doctor in the database (Section 7).
• Time and money: store date-times as UTC timestamps. Store money as integers in the smallest unit or as decimals, never floats, with a currency field that defaults to NPR.
• Audit log: the application's database role can insert into it but cannot update or delete from it.
• Indexes: add them for booking search (status, doctor, date, reference, profile), patient search and catalogue search.
• Import boundary: keep stable internal IDs and avoid clinic-software assumptions, so patient or catalogue data could be imported later.
11. Security, privacy and operations
The application holds personal and medical information. Apply these controls in proportion to the real risks, and in a form the clinic can operate.
Access control.
• Every protected read and write checks the session and the permission or ownership on the server. There are no exceptions for "internal" endpoints.
• Patient-facing queries always filter by the signed-in account's own profiles. For every patient-facing endpoint, test that a second patient account receives "not found".
• The browser never talks to the database or file storage directly.
• Use random identifiers in URLs, but never treat an unguessable URL as authorization.
Web protections.
• Validate every input on the server. Use parameterized queries only.
• Add CSRF protection and secure headers (Content-Security-Policy, X-Content-Type-Options, frame protection, referrer policy, and HSTS in production).
• Sanitize rich text from the CMS (blog, FAQ) before rendering it. Restrict image uploads by type and size.
• Rate-limit login, registration, password reset, booking submission, claim-code entry and report download. Keep the counters in the database or another shared store. Per-instance memory is not enough on hosts that run several instances.
Errors, logging and analytics.
• Show safe error messages with no stack traces, SQL, file paths or patient data.
• Logs never contain names, phone numbers, email addresses, health details, tokens, passwords or report contents. Log identifiers and a request ID instead.
• Add no analytics or third-party tracking scripts, and none at all on account, booking or CMS pages. Add analytics only if the clinic asks.
Audit. Record who did what and when for staff sign-ins and failed sign-ins, booking status changes, views and changes of patient records, claim-code issue and use, report actions (Section 8), staff-account and permission changes, and settings changes. Super Admin can view and filter the log. Nobody can edit it.
Secrets and environments. Keep every secret in environment variables or the platform's secret store, never in code, client bundles or logs. Provide .env.example with names only. Keep development and production separate. The sample-data seed must refuse to run when APP_ENV=production, and any development page showing sample data carries a visible "SAMPLE DATA" banner.
Backups and recovery. The app cannot back up infrastructure itself. In the README, document what is backed up (database and report storage), how often, where, who is responsible, how long copies are kept, and a step-by-step restore procedure with a test-restore drill the clinic repeats. Enable versioning or backup on report storage so an overwritten or deleted file can be recovered. Mark backups NOT CONFIGURED until the clinic confirms that its hosting plan provides them.
