12. UX, accessibility and SEO
Design direction. Make a calm, trustworthy, professional healthcare site: clean layouts, generous spacing, one consistent type scale and a restrained palette. Use a neutral placeholder palette until the supplied logo defines the brand colours. Design mobile-first, because many patients will use phones on slower connections. Use responsive images in modern formats, lazy-load images below the fold, subset fonts, and keep client-side JavaScript small on public pages.
Never invent awards, credentials, testimonials, ratings, staff photographs or facility images. Use the supplied logo and approved assets. Where an asset is missing, use a replaceable neutral placeholder that makes no claim about real people or facilities.
Usability.
• Booking forms have few fields, inline validation, messages in the current language, input preserved after an error, and a clear next step.
• Every list and page has loading, empty, success and error states.
• Patient screens use plain words, short explanations next to each status, and the "request, not confirmation" message.
• Reception screens are efficient: keyboard-friendly tables, filters, one-click common actions, confirmation for destructive actions, and clear conflict messages.
Accessibility. Target WCAG 2.2 AA. Use semantic HTML, labelled form controls with associated error messages, visible focus, a keyboard-operable navbar and dropdown, sufficient contrast, touch targets of at least 44 px, respect for reduced-motion settings, a skip link, and the correct lang attribute on each page (en or ne).
SEO and local discoverability.
• Public pages are server-rendered with search-friendly URLs.
• Each page has a title and description in each language, taken from CMS metadata with sensible fallbacks.
• Add canonical URLs, and hreflang only for translations that exist.
• The sitemap lists only published public pages, per language. robots.txt allows public pages and does not mention the CMS route.
• Account, booking, report, login and CMS pages carry noindex and Cache-Control: no-store.
• Emit structured data (MedicalClinic or LocalBusiness, and Physician for doctors) only from verified data in settings and the CMS, and only for fields that exist. Never emit rating or review markup.
• Add internal links between doctors, services, lab tests and related articles. Let staff choose related items in the CMS.
• Give images meaningful alt text in each language.
• Show the clinic's address, phone number, hours and map link consistently, from one settings source.
